{"Title":"An Act to protect the privacy of 911 callers","BillNumber":"H4323","DocketNumber":null,"GeneralCourtNumber":193,"PrimarySponsor":{"Id":"J19","Name":"Joint Committee on the Judiciary","Type":2,"Details":"https://malegislature.gov/api/GeneralCourts/193/Committees/J19","ResponseDate":"2024-02-05T12:25:30.813"},"Cosponsors":[{"Id":"MSD1","Name":"Michael S. Day","Type":1,"Details":"https://malegislature.gov/api/GeneralCourts/193/LegislativeMembers/MSD1","ResponseDate":"2023-01-20T15:22:39.2966667"},{"Id":"jml0","Name":"Jason M. Lewis","Type":1,"Details":"https://malegislature.gov/api/GeneralCourts/193/LegislativeMembers/jml0","ResponseDate":"2024-01-29T16:55:07.6"}],"JointSponsor":null,"BillHistory":"https://malegislature.gov/api/GeneralCourts/193/Documents/H4323/DocumentHistoryActions","LegislationTypeName":"Bill","Pinslip":null,"DocumentText":"\tSECTION 1. Chapter 6A of the Massachusetts General Laws is hereby amended by striking out section 18G and inserting in place thereof the following section:-\r\n\tSection 18G. (a) The department shall require that each PSAP that possesses enhanced 911 service shall retain 911 recordings for a period of not less than 1 year. \r\n\t(b) The audio recording of a call placed to a PSAP, regional emergency communication center or other 911 system for the purpose of requesting law enforcement, firefighting, medical, ambulance, emergency service provider or other emergency services shall not be deemed a public record under the provisions of section 7 of chapter 4 and shall be maintained in a manner that assures its confidentiality; provided, however, that the audio recording may be provided: (i) to the person who made the subject 911 call, their attorney or representative; (ii) to any person for whom emergency services were requested during the subject 911 call, their attorney or representative; (iii) to law enforcement agencies as defined in section 1 of chapter 6E for investigative purposes; (iv) to a prosecuting attorney, a defendant or a defendant’s attorney in a criminal proceeding involving facts or circumstances relating to the call; (v) with the express written consent of the person who made the subject call or the caller’s attorney; (vi) for public safety and emergency medical services training purposes; or (vii) pursuant to a court order finding that the right of the public to the release of the recording outweighs the privacy interests of the caller, the victim(s), or any person involved in the facts or circumstances relating to the 911 call.  A violation of this section shall be punishable by a fine of not more than $1,000.\r\n\t(c) A written transcript of the audio recording of a 911 call is a public record. A transcript shall be prepared upon request. The person requesting the transcript shall pay the actual cost of transcribing the call and any other costs related to transcript preparation. Before providing the transcript, the name, address, telephone number and other personally identifying information of the caller or any person involved in the facts or circumstances relating to the 911 call may be redacted.\r\n","EmergencyPreamble":null,"RollCalls":[],"Attachments":[],"CommitteeRecommendations":[{"Action":"Redraft","FiscalAmounts":[],"Committee":{"CommitteeCode":"J19","GeneralCourtNumber":193,"Details":"https://malegislature.gov/api/GeneralCourts/193/Committees/J19"},"Votes":[]}],"Amendments":[]}